      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Propofol injection pain is a common and unsolved anaesthesia problem."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The present study attempted to confirm that the plasticiser di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate in polyvinyl chloride (PVC) infusion tubes may increase propofol injection pain by increasing the aqueous propofol concentration."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A randomised controlled study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University teaching hospital, 1 April to 25 June 2013."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "One hundred patients scheduled for elective surgery were allocated randomly to the PVC or the control (C) group. The PVC group received a propofol (Diprivan) infusion via a 1-m PVC infusion extension tube, whereas group C received propofol injected directly through the port of the cannula."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "After the syringe was loaded with propofol, air was expelled from the tube and the syringe was left standing for 5\u200amin; intravenous propofol 0.5\u200amg\u200akg was then injected either through the PVC tube or directly into the cannula."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE",
          "content": "A verbal rating scale was used to evaluate the propofol injection pain in both groups. Di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate and aqueous propofol concentrations were also measured in samples of propofol after simulated injection. To investigate whether the increase in aqueous propofol concentration was caused by leached di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate, the same amount of di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate as that measured in the PVC group was added to the samples (group D)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The incidences of pain in groups PVC and C were 88 and 46%, respectively (P\u200a<\u200a0.0001). The di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate concentration in group PVC (1.01\u200a±\u200a0.07\u200aμg\u200aml) was greater than that in group C (lower than the detection limit of 0.03\u200aμg\u200aml). No significant difference was found between the aqueous propofol concentrations in groups PVC (25.9\u200a±\u200a1.8\u200aμg\u200aml) and D (24.4\u200a±\u200a1.1\u200aμg\u200aml) (P\u200a=\u200a0.22), which were significantly higher than that in group C (14.3\u200a±\u200a1.0\u200aμg\u200aml) (P\u200a=\u200a0.079)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Propofol injection pain is increased by contact with PVC infusion tubing as a result of an increase in aqueous propofol concentration caused by di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate leaching into the lipid emulsion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION",
          "content": "chictr.org identifier: ChiCTR-TRC-12003170."
        }
      ]},
